# hope-vllm-config — vLLM engine sizing and weight source (TASK-823)
#
# Consumed by base/vllm.yaml via NON-OPTIONAL configMapKeyRefs. A missing key
# here fails the pod as CreateContainerConfigError, which is deliberate: a
# silently-unset value would be passed to vLLM as the literal string
# "$(VLLM_MODEL_URI)".
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# EVERY NUMBER BELOW IS DERIVED, NOT CHOSEN. The derivation is TASK-823 §2A.
# Short form, for the reader who is about to change one of them:
#
#   Card:  NVIDIA RTX 2000 Ada, 16380 MiB (15.99 GiB), 2 of them, one node.
#          No MIG, no MPS, no vGPU — time-slicing only, which provides NO VRAM
#          isolation and no way to reserve a whole card.
#
#   Worst-case co-tenancy on the card this pod lands on:
#          hope-stt ~3.9 GiB + hope-stt-worker ~3.7 GiB = 7.6 GiB taken.
#          15.99 - 7.6 - ~0.4 (driver ctx) = ~8.0 GiB left for vLLM.
#          8.0 / 15.99 = 0.50  ->  VLLM_GPU_MEMORY_UTILIZATION
#
#   KV cache per token = 2 * layers * kv_heads * head_dim * dtype_bytes.
#          For a Qwen3-class model (36 layers, 8 KV heads, head_dim 128, BF16):
#          2 * 36 * 8 * 128 * 2 = 147,456 B = 144 KiB/token.
#
#   KV budget at 0.50 with Qwen/Qwen3-4B-AWQ (weights MEASURED, not estimated:
#   model.safetensors is 2,666,027,672 B = 2.483 GiB. TASK-823 §2A estimated
#   3.19 GiB because it assumed UNTIED embeddings; this checkpoint sets
#   tie_word_embeddings=true, so the 151936x2560 fp16 table is stored once,
#   saving 0.724 GiB):
#          8.0 GiB - 2.483 (weights) - 1.0 (activations + CUDA graphs)
#            = 4.52 GiB = 4.85e9 B
#          4.85e9 / 147,456 = ~32,860 tokens of KV
#          / 4096 max-model-len = ~8.0 concurrent sequences.
#
# THAT IS THE HONEST CAPACITY OF THIS HARDWARE: about EIGHT concurrent
# generations, against a ticket target of 20-40. Do not raise
# VLLM_GPU_MEMORY_UTILIZATION to close that gap. Under time-slicing the sum
# across co-scheduled pods is enforced by nothing, so a higher value does not
# buy capacity — it buys a CUDA OOM whose victim is chosen by allocation order,
# and the likely victim is hope-stt.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── model identity ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# An s3:// URI that runai_streamer reads directly from MinIO — there is no
# download step and no PVC. Under the MLflow promotion path (TASK-822) CI
# resolves `models:/<name>@champion` to an immutable URI and commits it HERE;
# the pod never talks to MLflow.
#
# The 4B-class AWQ checkpoint is the ONLY size that fits the arithmetic above.
# The chosen checkpoint is `Qwen/Qwen3-4B-AWQ` — an OFFICIAL Qwen release (not a
# community requant), AWQ W4A16 gemm / group_size 128, ONE model.safetensors
# shard, sha256 a7043493ebd993f5fea18794ad7b5b3e064a52023f392a7fcce7ce0984c341f0.
# A single unsharded file keeps the streamer's job trivial and makes the
# checksum a single value to carry into MLflow.
#
# For reference, the single vLLM AiModel row currently in the seed catalogue
# (`vllm-medgemma-1.5-27b-it`) declares memorySizeMb 55296 — 54 GiB, roughly
# three and a half of these cards for weights alone. It cannot run here.
#
# The trailing slash is OPTIONAL — tested, not assumed. Both `.../v1` and
# `.../v1/` resolve to the same single shard even with a decoy `.../v10/`
# prefix present in the same bucket, so `list_safetensors` normalises the
# prefix rather than doing a bare string match. Kept here for readability only.
VLLM_MODEL_URI=s3://hope-models/qwen3-4b-awq/v1/

# MUST equal AiModel.sourceUri of the row that the SYSTEM AiTaskDefault for
# text.live / text.finalize points at. apps/text puts AiModel.sourceUri on the
# wire as the OpenAI `model` field, so a mismatch here is a 404 on every
# generation rather than a startup error. See ../README.md §5.
VLLM_SERVED_MODEL_NAME=qwen3-4b-awq

# ── sizing (see the derivation above before editing) ─────────────────────────
# NEVER the model's advertised maximum. Advertised max multiplies KV per
# sequence and collapses concurrency — TASK-823 pitfall #1.
VLLM_MAX_MODEL_LEN=4096

# Fraction of the WHOLE card, not of what happens to be free.
VLLM_GPU_MEMORY_UTILIZATION=0.50

# A CEILING, not achieved concurrency (pitfall #2). The KV arithmetic above
# lands at ~8.0 sequences with the MEASURED weight size, so 8 is the ceiling
# that matches it; the KV cache is what actually bounds throughput. Read the
# engine's own `GPU KV cache size: N tokens` line at startup and divide by
# VLLM_MAX_MODEL_LEN before trusting this number — the activation/CUDA-graph
# term is the least precise input to the estimate.
VLLM_MAX_NUM_SEQS=8

# vLLM constraints: >= 2048, and >= max_num_seqs. Matching max-model-len keeps
# a full-length prefill in a single batch whether or not chunked prefill is on.
VLLM_MAX_NUM_BATCHED_TOKENS=4096

# ── weight source (MinIO / S3) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE LAN ADDRESS, NOT THE PUBLIC TUNNEL HOSTNAME. Resolved in Phase 2.
#
# `hope-secrets.MINIO_ENDPOINT` is `s3.taphuynh.dev`, a PROXIED CLOUDFLARE
# TUNNEL hostname with no Cloudflare Access application (TASK-828 §4). The
# tunnel's own route table says where that actually lands:
#
#     s3.taphuynh.dev          ->  https://10.10.1.102:9000   (noTLSVerify: true)
#     s3-console.taphuynh.dev  ->  https://10.10.1.102:9001
#
# So MinIO is a dedicated LAN host on the SAME /24 as the k3s node (10.10.1.10).
# There is NO MinIO Service, Endpoints or Pod anywhere in the cluster — it is
# out-of-band infrastructure, exactly like Postgres (10.10.1.250:5000) and
# Redis (10.10.1.120:6379), both of which hope-secrets ALREADY addresses by raw
# LAN IP. MinIO was the only one going out through the internet.
#
# MEASURED from inside hope-v2-dev (hope-text pod, GET /minio/health/live, n=7):
#     LAN     10.10.1.102:9000      median   2.0 ms   (min 1.9,  max 8.3)
#     TUNNEL  s3.taphuynh.dev:443   median 495.6 ms   (min 387,  max 1452)
# ~248x per request. The Run:ai streamer issues many concurrent ranged GETs, so
# that multiplies straight into cold-start time — and every byte of a multi-GB
# weight load would leave the PHI namespace for the public internet and come
# back. Using the LAN address also makes the egress NetworkPolicy expressible
# as a single /32 (see base/vllm.yaml), which it simply is not against
# Cloudflare's edge ranges.
#
# ── TRANSPORT: HTTPS, VERIFICATION DISABLED — OWNER DIRECTIVE 2026-08-30 ─────
# The scheme stays `https://`. MinIO serves TLS on :9000 and ONE PORT SERVES ONE
# SCHEME, so switching this workload to plain HTTP would have forced pgBackRest,
# GitLab, Loki, Tempo, Prometheus and both cloudflared origins to be re-pointed
# as collateral. That is why the endpoint below is unchanged from Phase 2.
#
# What DID change is TRUST. The owner directed that the private "ARCAAI Internal
# CA" be removed from this workload ENTIRELY — no ConfigMap, no bundle, no mount
# — and that AUTHENTICATION be a MinIO SERVICE ACCOUNT (access key + secret) and
# nothing else. So certificate verification is EXPLICITLY TURNED OFF rather than
# pointed at a CA. The accompanying directive is that PHI hardening is explicitly
# DE-PRIORITISED for now.
#
# ⚠️ THIS IS A DELIBERATE, REVERSIBLE RELAXATION. An unverified TLS connection
# encrypts the wire but does NOT authenticate the peer, so it does not defend
# against an on-path attacker on the 10.10.1.0/24 LAN — it only defends against
# passive capture. The egress NetworkPolicy still confines this traffic to a
# single /32. REINSTATEMENT IS ONE CHANGE: restore `AWS_CA_BUNDLE` +
# `VLLM_S3_CA_BUNDLE` + the `arcaai-internal-ca` mount (the full procedure is
# preserved verbatim in ../README.md §9.1) and drop the verify-off setting in
# base/vllm.yaml.
VLLM_S3_ENDPOINT_URL=https://10.10.1.102:9000

# ⚠️⚠️ OPEN-823-TLS — READ BEFORE ENABLING THIS WORKLOAD. Not a warning; a
# MEASUREMENT that says this configuration does not currently work.
#
# There are TWO S3 clients in vLLM's weight-load path and only one of them has a
# documented "skip verification" control:
#
#   pull_files() / list_safetensors()  — config, tokenizer, shard listing
#       -> boto3 / botocore (Python). Reads AWS_CA_BUNDLE. It has **NO
#          environment variable that disables verification**; `verify=False` is
#          a client-CONSTRUCTION argument and vLLM constructs the client itself,
#          so a manifest cannot reach it.
#   SafetensorsStreamer.stream_files()  — the weights
#       -> Run:ai C++ / AWS C++ SDK. Reads RUNAI_STREAMER_* (see base/vllm.yaml).
#
# Phase 2 measured variant 2 — "https:// endpoint, private CA, NO AWS_CA_BUNDLE"
# — and it FAILED with `botocore.exceptions.SSLError … CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED`
# on the FIRST LIST (../README.md §10). That is exactly this configuration.
#
# So unless the MinIO leaf is signed by a publicly-trusted CA, or the CA is
# restored, vLLM is expected to fail at startup. Three ways out, all owner
# decisions and none of them takeable inside this manifest:
#   (a) reinstate the CA for vLLM only (R-1, ../README.md §9.1);
#   (b) put a publicly-trusted certificate on the MinIO listener;
#   (c) patch vLLM's loader to honour a verify-off setting (upstream change).
# This is NOT blocking today: the workload ships at replicas: 0 for an unrelated
# hardware reason. It becomes blocking the moment someone scales it to 1.
#
# NOTE: there is deliberately NO CA-bundle key here any more. `VLLM_S3_CA_BUNDLE`
# and the `arcaai-internal-ca` ConfigMap it named were REMOVED on 2026-08-30 by
# owner directive; the `ROOT_CONFIG_REQUEST R-1` that asked for that ConfigMap is
# CANCELLED, not silently dropped — see ../README.md §9.1.

# NOTE: there is deliberately no region key here. MinIO ignores the region and
# the AWS SDK only needs one to be set, so base/vllm.yaml carries AWS_REGION as
# a literal rather than a non-optional ConfigMap ref that could fail the pod for
# a value that never legitimately varies.
